+**The lead's note for this run (beta round 3, 2026-10-16).** You run in your own git worktree. First run `git fetch origin claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp && git checkout --detach origin/claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp`. Symlink today's crawl into it: `ln -s /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/wt-data2/data/snapshots data/snapshots; mkdir -p data/beta; ln -s /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/wt-data2/data/beta/snapshots data/beta/snapshots` (never commit them). The data is the 2026-10-16 crawl with every board whole, no cap: Amazon 22,179 postings, Apple 4,851, Google 3,821, NVIDIA 2,711, Microsoft 2,435, Meta 1,208, Netflix 414; main site Anduril 2,537. The lead's reviewer build is /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/cycle-beta6-dist; serve it and don't rebuild it. What changed since the last review: one-line notes with a Details disclosure (fix-density), Google grouped by job family with a Company filter (google-grouping), the salary audit fixes ("· 2 levels", per-place pay, no cut rows), first-page preview and a faster first render (perf-mobile-beta-2), and fix6-fresh-data. Read those sections of docs/process/lead.md first. Resume safety (the user, 2026-10-16: "if we get stop again due to limits, we're able to resume without losing much progress"): commit your files small and often in your worktree with explicit paths, keep a `## State` section at the top of your log, and send the lead your findings as you confirm them by SendMessage to "main". The lead checkpoints your worktree every 15 minutes; never push. Where the template says "don't commit", commit in your worktree anyway; the lead integrates. Give every server, browser or test command a timeout, so nothing can hang for hours. Other agents may run at the same time on a 4-CPU machine: note `uptime` with any timing. Check especially what changed since round 2: Amazon's whole board (22,179 postings) and Meta; the first-page preview on Amazon (real cards under "Loading 22,179 roles…", then the list replaces them); Google's job-family departments with the Company group-by and filter; the one-line notes with one Details disclosure (fix-density); "· 2 levels" and "· N places" cards, "from €X" minimums and shift-pay labels; the clusters chart, whose circles now sit at their postings' median pay, and the Ranges view, where identical posted bands are stacked into one row with ×N (viz-even). Also re-check that earlier findings stay fixed. Known open items you need not re-report, but may add evidence to: MR-23 (FX rates from 2026-07-01, waiting on the user), MR-26, MR-28, MR-29 (the on-screen label), MR-33 to MR-35, MR-37 to MR-42 in docs/process/methodology-review-beta-2.md, and the Insights tab on Amazon being one 4.3 s task (docs/process/perf-mobile-beta-2.md).
+
+You are the lead UX designer and frontend reviewer for melon-seek (/home/user/melon-seek), "the Zillow of job postings": a company's job board plotted on a salary chart or a map, with Zillow-like filters, a Juice Score (pay left after tax, rent and living costs), Compstimate and Insights. It is a zero-build vanilla-JS app. Read README.md, docs/strategy/ROADMAP.md §8 (the UI simplicity budget), docs/CONTRACT.md, docs/METHODOLOGY_GUIDE.md, docs/process/ux.md (how the UI was designed), docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md, docs/process/TEMPLATE.md and the newest sections of docs/process/lead.md (from "Beta round 3" on: fix wave 5, fix-density, google-grouping, the salary audit, perf-mobile-beta-2, fix6-fresh-data), docs/process/methodology-review-beta.md, docs/process/ux-review-beta.md, docs/process/viz-review-beta.md and the 2026-10-11 beta sections of docs/QA.md.
+
+**Your job:** a design and frontend review of target `beta` (`/melon-seek/beta/`), judged as a senior product designer would: is it clear, consistent, calm and honest, and does it hold up with real data? The QA agents test whether features work and the persona reviewers play job seekers; you look at the design itself. Do not edit product code. You own:
+- docs/process/ux-review-beta-2.md (your log and findings);
+- docs/process/prompts/ux-review-beta-2.md (paste this prompt verbatim);
+- screenshots in docs/screenshots/ux-review-beta-2/ (before you report, run `bash scripts/cycle/shrink-screenshots.sh docs/screenshots/ux-review-beta-2`);
+- a NEW test file, test/e2e/beta-ux-review-2.e2e.js, registered the way scripts/e2e.js registers the others. Every objective problem (text overflowing or clipped, overlapping elements, horizontal scroll, a control hidden behind another, contrast under 4.5:1 for body text) gets a test that fails now and will pass once it is fixed. Put the finding ID in the test name, e.g. `[UX-3]`. Matters of taste go in the log, not in tests.
+Don't commit and never push.
+
+**Setup:** serve the build the lead made: `node scripts/cycle/serve.mjs --dist /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/cycle-beta6-dist --pid /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/ux-review-beta-2/serve.pid`. Don't rebuild it; other reviewers use it at the same time. Playwright: NODE_PATH=/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/pw/node_modules, Chromium at /opt/pw-browsers/chromium-1194/chrome-linux/chrome; never run `playwright install`. Viewports 1440x900, 1280x800, 1024x768, 768x1024 and 390x844 (isMobile, hasTouch), light and dark. Use pid files; never `pkill -f` or `pgrep -f`. Boards: all seven big-tech boards (Amazon, Apple, Google, Meta, Microsoft, NVIDIA and Netflix), plus Anduril from the main site for comparison.
+
+**Review, with a screenshot for every finding:**
+1. **First impression.** In the first 10 seconds, can a newcomer tell what the page is, what the chart shows and what to do next? What draws the eye first, and should it?
+2. **Visual hierarchy and layout.** Top bar, filter bar, list, chart or map, drawer. Spacing rhythm, alignment, grouping, density. Does the layout hold with thousands of roles (Apple), long titles, long local place names, many departments, empty filter results?
+3. **Consistency.** The same thing named the same way everywhere (pay, salary, compensation; level, seniority; posting, role, job). The same number formatted the same way (currency, "K", ranges, per year/month/hour). Buttons, chips, toggles and popovers behave alike. Compare against the main site (`/melon-seek/`) and list differences that are not intended.
+4. **Words.** Every label, tooltip, empty state and caption: plain English, no jargon, no abbreviation that is not explained where it appears (ROADMAP §8). Quote each bad string with its location and a suggested rewrite.
+5. **States.** Loading, empty, error, "Pay unclear", estimate vs posted pay, Snapshot vs Live badge, beta notice. Is each one clear about what happened and what to do?
+6. **Text density (a hard check, not taste).** On a 390x844 and a 360x740 phone and at 1440 on desktop, for every board, read every line of secondary text: the chart notes and caption, the stats line, the map notes and legend, the results header, the drawer's pay sub-lines, the Insights and Compstimate captions, empty states and the badge. Record each line's full text, word count and how many lines it wraps to. Any line over one line on a phone, or over about 12 visible words, or that strings more than two facts together, is a finding (Medium at least; High if it pushes the main content off the first screen). Give a rewrite that keeps one or two facts on the line and moves the rest behind one details disclosure. Encode each as a test that fails now. (The user, 2026-10-11, about a chart notes line of six facts: "that's way too busy and too much on a mobile. Make sure you UI/UX QC really do the job and not just gloss experiences like this over.")
+7. **Simplicity budget.** Count the visible controls on first load per viewport and compare with §8. Anything new since the newest sections of docs/process/lead.md (from "Beta round 3" on: fix wave 5, fix-density, google-grouping, the salary audit, perf-mobile-beta-2, fix6-fresh-data), docs/process/methodology-review-beta.md, docs/process/ux-review-beta.md, docs/process/viz-review-beta.md and the 2026-10-11 beta sections of docs/QA.md that should be folded away or removed?
+8. **Responsive and themes.** Every viewport in light and dark: clipping, overlap, wrapping, tap targets under 44 px on the phone, text under 12 px, contrast (measure it; quote the ratio).
+9. **Motion and feedback.** Transitions, focus rings, hover states, what happens after a click (is there visible feedback within 100 ms?), reduced motion.
+10. **Honesty of presentation.** Pay ranges that span several levels, estimates, posted pay that covers only some places, currency conversions: does the design make the caveat visible where the number is, not only in a tooltip?
+11. **Beta only:** the beta notice, the Snapshot badge, the links back to the main site, and whether big-tech postings (department names, store roles, level ranges, non-US currencies) look as polished as the main site's.
+
+For each finding write: an ID (UX-n, numbered after the highest UX ID in docs/process/), a severity (High: misleads or blocks; Medium: confuses or looks broken; Low: polish), the viewport and theme, the screen, a screenshot, what is wrong, a concrete fix (copy text, CSS or layout change), and the likely owning file and function. Say what works well too. Also run `npm test` and the full e2e suite, and report the results.
+
+Report back with the findings ordered by severity, one line each, then the three best things to keep.
